220,18,1124,408,90,5187,2555,2137,3,323,57960,7265,90,5187,2555,92,220,16,24984,481,18,1124,408,90,5187,2555,31716,311,57960,7265,90,5187,2555,92,220,22,24984,481,16,1124,408,90,5187,2555,92,2418,220,7379,279,2168,315,279,1555,400,88,284,220,17,87,488,220,16,3,1212,57960,10374,13233,90,44,92,2418,220,17399,697,4226,304,279,1352,5201,88,284,14619,488,293,3,3263,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nA matrix $\\mathbf{M}$ takes $\\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}$ to $\\begin{pmatrix} 9 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix},$ and $\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix}$ to $\\begin{pmatrix} 7 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}.$  Find the image of the line $y = 2x + 1$ under $\\mathbf{M}.$  Express your answer in the form \"$y = mx + b$\".","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"121135392e01aa03af7cf7c3784890ae79183a4897d6b6ca20c0f6a913d6a50a"},"seconds":73.70507073402405,"seed":917286888522781013,"stop":"eos","text":"To find the image of the line \\(y = 2x + 1\\) under the matrix transformation \\(\\mathbf{M}\\), we need to determine the matrix \\(\\mathbf{M}\\) and then apply it to a general point on the line.\n\nWe know that \\(\\mathbf{M} \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 9 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\\) and \\(\\mathbf{M} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 7 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\\). Let's denote \\(\\mathbf{M} = \\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ c & d \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\nFrom the first condition, we have:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ c & d \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 9 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\nThis gives us two equations:\n\\[\n2a - b = 9 \\quad \\text{(1)}\n\\]\n\\[\n2c - d = 3 \\quad \\text{(2)}\n\\]\n\nFrom the second condition, we have:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ c & d \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 7 \\\\ -1 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\nThis gives us two more equations:\n\\[\na - 3b = 7 \\quad \\text{(3)}\n\\]\n\\[\nc - 3d = -1 \\quad \\text{(4)}\n\\]\n\nWe now solve these systems of equations. First, solve for \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) using equations (1) and (3):\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n2a - b = 9 \\\\\na - 3b = 7\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nMultiply the second equation by 2:\n\\[\n2a - 6b = 14\n\\]\nSubtract this from the first equation:\n\\[\n(2a - b) - (2a - 6b) = 9 - 14\n\\]\n\\[\n5b = -5 \\implies b = -1\n\\]\nSubstitute \\(b = -1\\) into equation (1):\n\\[\n2a + 1 = 9 \\implies 2a = 8 \\implies a = 4\n\\]\n\nNext, solve for \\(c\\) and \\(d\\) using equations (2) and (4):\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n2c - d = 3 \\\\\nc - 3d = -1\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nMultiply the second equation by 2:\n\\[\n2c - 6d = -2\n\\]\nSubtract this from the first equation:\n\\[\n(2c - d) - (2c - 6d) = 3 + 2\n\\]\n\\[\n5d = 5 \\implies d = 1\n\\]\nSubstitute \\(d = 1\\) into equation (2):\n\\[\n2c - 1 = 3 \\implies 2c = 4 \\implies c = 2\n\\]\n\nThus, the matrix \\(\\mathbf{M}\\) is:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{M} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nNow, we apply \\(\\mathbf{M}\\) to a general point \\((x, 2x + 1)\\) on the line \\(y = 2x + 1\\):\n\\[\n\\mathbf{M} \\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ 2x + 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ 2x + 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4x - (2x + 1) \\\\ 2x + (2x + 1) \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2x - 1 \\\\ 4x + 1 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\nLet the new coordinates be \\((X, Y)\\). Then,\n\\[\nX = 2x - 1 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad Y = 4x + 1\n\\]\nFrom \\(X = 2x - 1\\), we solve for \\(x\\):\n\\[\nx = \\frac{X + 1}{2}\n\\]\nSubstitute \\(x\\) into the equation for \\(Y\\):\n\\[\nY = 4 \\left( \\frac{X + 1}{2} \\right) + 1 = 2(X + 1) + 1 = 2X + 2 + 1 = 2X + 3\n\\]\n\nThus, the image of the line \\(y = 2x + 1\\) under the matrix \\(\\mathbf{M}\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{y = 2x + 3}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{y = 2x + 3}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
